[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 764,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 701,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119683,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 701,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>B-lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma (B-ALL/LBL) with IGH::<em>IL3</em> fusion is a neoplasm of lymphoblasts of B&nbsp;lineage defined by the juxtaposition of the IGH enhancer and the <em>IL3</em> promoter, with characteristic peripheral blood and bone marrow eosinophilia.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 765,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 702,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119684,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 702,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>9817/3 B-lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma with IGH::<em>IL3</em> fusion</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 766,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 703,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119685,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 703,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>2A70.Y &amp; XH4ZL2 Other B-lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma with recurrent genetic abnormalities &amp; B-lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma with t(5;14)(q31;q32); <em>IL3</em>-IGH</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 767,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 704,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119686,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 704,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 768,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 705,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119687,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 705,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 769,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 706,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119688,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 706,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>B-ALL and B-LBL represent the same disease. B-ALL with IGH::<em>IL3</em> fusion always involves the bone marrow and usually also involves the peripheral blood. Extramedullary involvement is common and is by definition the primary site of involvement in B-LBL. For details, see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/63/385\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>B-lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma</em> . Eosinophilic infiltrates can involve solid organs, including the lungs, heart (Loeffler endocarditis), and CNS, even without infiltration by leukaemic blasts {25382309; 30207070; 31921638}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 770,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 707,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119689,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 707,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The clinical presentation can be quite variable, including a typical presentation of B-ALL/LBL with increased blasts; asymptomatic eosinophilia with low or absent peripheral blood blasts; or clinical manifestations of hypereosinophilic syndrome with organomegaly, dyspnoea, skin involvement, neurological symptoms, thromboembolic events, and eosinophilic cardiomyopathy {6583469; 31921638; 28729339}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 771,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 708,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119690,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 708,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>This is a very rare type (&lt;&nbsp;1%) of B-ALL/LBL, with only case reports or small case series described in the literature. Most reported cases are in children and young adults (median age: 14.3&nbsp;years), with a male predominance {31921638}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 772,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 709,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119691,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 709,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 773,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 710,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119692,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 710,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The IGH::<em>IL3</em> fusion is most commonly due to a balanced t(5;14)(q31.1;q32) that joins the IGH enhancer (14q32) to the<em>IL3</em> gene promoter (5q31.1) {2114933; 2499362}. This results in IL-3 overexpression that drives the leukaemic clone in an autocrine manner and induces eosinophil maturation in the bone marrow and reactive eosinophilia in the peripheral blood {7512364; 30207070}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 780,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 717,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119693,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 717,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 774,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 711,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119694,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 711,
        "headingText": "<p>The bone marrow may show partial infiltration by lymphoblasts in a background of increased eosinophils. Peripheral blood blasts may be low, with a normal or slightly decreased haemoglobin and platelet count {31921638; 26662494}.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Immunophenotype</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>The lymphoblasts show typical expression of CD19 and CD10. A subset of cases shows expression of myeloid markers, CD33 and/or CD13 {15916678; 31921638}. Both lymphoblasts and eosinophils express the IL-3 receptor, CD123 {30207070}.</p>\r\n&nbsp;",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-21T11:55:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 775,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 712,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119695,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 712,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Peripheral blood and bone marrow aspirate smears commonly show increased eosinophils and variable numbers of lymphoblasts.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 776,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 713,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119696,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 713,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The t(5;14)(q31.1;q32) translocation can be detected by karyotyping or FISH, but detection can be challenging in cases with a low blast count or a cytogenetically cryptic rearrangement. FISH for IGH rearrangement can be applied, but it is not diagnostic. Next-generation sequencing assays may identify IGH::<em>IL3</em> fusion with greater sensitivity {33991782; 31921638}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 779,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 716,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119697,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 716,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> meets the diagnostic criteria for B-ALL/LBL (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/63/385\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>B-lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma</em>); demonstration of IGH::<em>IL3</em> fusion.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 777,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 714,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119698,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 714,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 778,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 715,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 119699,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 715,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>There are too few cases to be certain about the prognosis. A small case series suggested an intermediate prognosis, with a poor response to treatment and high levels of measurable residual disease at the end of induction {31921638}. Blast percentage at diagnosis is not considered to be a prognostic factor. An increase in eosinophils during and after treatment may suggest relapsed disease.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T09:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]